// Dictionnaire français -> anglais. Les longues définitions sont référencées par leur identifiant
// (DEFINITIONS.sharpe…) pour ne jamais dépendre d'une recopie exacte du texte français.
import { DEFINITIONS as D, BOND_DEFS as B, GREEKS as G, SCREENER_METRICS as SM } from '../definitions';
import { STRATEGIES } from '../finance/optionsMath';

const DEF_EN = {
  total_change: 'Price change between the first and last day of the period. Prices are adjusted: dividends and stock splits are taken into account.',
  cagr: "Compound annual growth rate (CAGR): the constant yearly return that would have taken you from the starting point to the end point. Useful to compare periods of different lengths. Over one year it is almost equal to the total change; over less than a year it is an extrapolation.",
  sharpe: "How much the asset earned above a risk-free investment, for each unit of risk taken. Formula: (annualised return − risk-free rate) ÷ annualised volatility. The risk-free rate is that of the asset's currency (US Treasury bill for the dollar, ECB rate for the euro…), shown at the top of the table. Rules of thumb: < 0 poor, 0.5–1 decent, > 1 very good, > 2 exceptional.",
  sortino: 'A variant of Sharpe that only counts downside volatility as risk: sharp rises are not penalised. More relevant for assets with sudden jumps.',
  calmar: 'CAGR ÷ |Max Drawdown|. Compares the average annual gain with the worst drop. Above 1, one average year is enough to erase the worst fall.',
  omega: "Add up all the daily rises on one side and all the falls on the other: Omega is the ratio between the two. Above 1, the balance is positive. Unlike Sharpe, it accounts for the real size of good and bad days. (Traders also call it the “Profit Factor”: it is the same calculation.)",
  win_rate: "Percentage of sessions that closed higher (flat sessions excluded). Read it with the Omega ratio: you can win small often and lose big rarely.",
  mdd: "Max Drawdown: the largest fall between a peak and the lowest point that followed. Imagine buying at the worst moment (the peak) and selling at the worst moment (the low): that is your loss. A fall always takes a bigger rise to recover: after −50%, it takes +100% to get back.",
  mean_dd: "A drawdown is the gap between today's price and the last record reached (0% on record days). The average drawdown averages this gap over all days when the asset was below a record: it is the “usual” dip felt while holding it, less extreme than the Max Drawdown.",
  current_dd: "Gap between the last price and the asset's all-time high, whatever the period chosen (for a portfolio: the high of the simulated period). 0% = the asset is at a record.",
  recovery: "Average length of the periods spent below a previous record: from the record until the day the asset sets a new one. These are not consecutive down days: the asset can rise and fall in the meantime, as long as it stays below its record. A period not yet over is counted up to today.",
  ulcer: "The Ulcer Index measures how stressful an asset is to hold: it rises when falls are deep and when they last. Two assets can have the same volatility, but the one that stays far from its record for a long time is much harder to keep (hence the name). Formula: square root of the mean of squared drawdowns. Rules of thumb: < 5 calm, > 15 trying.",
  vol: "Volatility measures the usual size of price swings, up and down (standard deviation of daily returns, scaled to a year: × √252, or × √365 for crypto which trades every day). The higher it is, the more the price yo-yos.",
  downside_vol: "Volatility computed from down days only, scaled to a year. It only measures the risk that hurts. If it is clearly above total volatility ÷ 1.4, falls are sharper than rises.",
  var_h: '1-day historical Value at Risk: loss threshold that was exceeded in only (100 − confidence level)% of observed sessions. It is not the maximum loss: the worst days go further (see CVaR).',
  var_p: "1-day parametric Value at Risk: same threshold as the historical VaR, but computed assuming daily moves follow a bell curve (normal distribution). Formula: mean − z × standard deviation, where z is the number of standard deviations matching the confidence level (1.645 at 95%, 2.326 at 99%). It often underestimates crash risk, because very bad days are more frequent than the bell curve predicts.",
  cvar: "CVaR = Conditional Value at Risk, also called Expected Shortfall. It is the average loss on the sessions that go beyond the historical VaR. It answers: “when things go wrong, how much do we lose on average?”",
  beta: 'Sensitivity to benchmark moves (covariance ÷ benchmark variance). 1 = follows the market, > 1 = amplifies, < 1 = dampens, < 0 = moves the opposite way.',
  alpha: "Alpha measures how much more (or less) the asset earned than expected given its link with the benchmark (its beta). Example: with a beta of 1.2, if the index beats the risk-free rate by 10%, you expect the asset to beat it by about 12%; if the asset did 3 points better, its alpha is +3%. Positive = it beat what the market implied. (Method: Jensen's alpha, from the CAPM.)",
  correlation: 'Correlation of daily returns with the benchmark, from −1 (opposite moves) to +1 (identical moves). The lower it is, the more the asset diversifies a portfolio invested in the benchmark.',
  r_squared: "R² tells what share of the asset's moves goes hand in hand with those of the benchmark (0% = no link, 100% = the asset moves exactly like the index). It measures a common movement, not a cause: two assets can move together because they react to the same news (interest rates, risk appetite).",
  tracking_error: "Each day, the asset does a little better or worse than the benchmark. Tracking error measures the size of these gaps over a year. Low (under 3%) for an ETF that tracks the index, high for an asset with a life of its own.",
  information_ratio: 'Annualised outperformance versus the benchmark ÷ tracking error. Measures how consistent the outperformance is: > 0.5 good, > 1 excellent.',
  capture: 'Up / down capture: share of the benchmark’s average rise (or fall) that the asset records on the same days. Ideal: high up capture, low down capture.',
  skew: 'Skewness of daily returns. Negative: big surprises tend to be drops. Positive: tend to be surges. 0 = symmetric.',
  kurt: 'Excess kurtosis: thickness of the distribution tails compared with a normal distribution (which scores 0). A high value signals extreme days (crashes or surges) more often than expected.',
  tail_ratio: '95th percentile ÷ |5th percentile| of daily returns: compares the size of the best days with the worst.',
  positive_months: 'Share of calendar months that ended higher, with the best and worst month of the period.',
  best_day: "Largest one-session rise over the period, with its date.",
  worst_day: "Largest one-session fall over the period, with its date.",
  mean_daily: 'Average return per session (arithmetic mean).',
  monte_carlo: '5,000 future paths are simulated from the historical trend and volatility (geometric Brownian motion). Pessimistic = 5th percentile (5% of simulations end lower), median = central scenario, optimistic = 95th percentile. Strong assumption: the future statistically resembles the past, and extreme crashes are under-represented. This is not a forecast.',
  return_contribution: "How many points of the portfolio's return come from this line. Formula: starting weight × the asset's return over the period. Example: 20% of the portfolio in an asset that gained +50% contributes +10 points. Added up, the contributions give the return of a portfolio bought at the start and left untouched (with rebalancing, the total can differ slightly). A negative contribution flags a line that lost the portfolio money.",
  risk_contribution: 'Share of portfolio risk (variance) due to this line. Accounts for its weight, its volatility and its correlation with the other lines. A line can be 10% of the capital and 30% of the risk.',
  diversification_ratio: 'Weighted average of individual volatilities ÷ portfolio volatility. 1 = no diversification; the higher it is, the more the lines offset each other.',
  average_correlation: 'Average correlation between all pairs of assets in the portfolio. Below 0.3: good diversification; above 0.7: the assets move almost together.',
};

const BOND_EN = {
  curve: 'Yield curve: yield on US government debt by maturity. Normally upward-sloping (lending longer is riskier). When inverted (short rates > long rates), it has preceded most US recessions.',
  s10_2: 'Gap between the 10-year and 2-year yields. Negative = inverted curve: the market expects rate cuts, often because of an expected slowdown.',
  credit: 'Risk premium (OAS) demanded from companies over government debt. It widens when investors fear defaults and tightens when they are confident.',
  price: 'Bond price, as a % of face value. Above 100: the coupon is higher than market rates; below: it is lower.',
  ytm: 'Yield to maturity (YTM): annual return earned if you buy at this price, collect every coupon (reinvested at the same rate) and hold the bond to maturity.',
  current: 'Current yield: annual coupon ÷ price. Ignores the gain or loss at maturity (redemption at 100).',
  macaulay: 'Macaulay duration: average life of the cash flows, weighted by their present value (in years).',
  modified: 'Modified duration: approximate price change (in %) for a 1-point move in rates. 7 = the price falls about 7% if rates rise 1%.',
  convexity: 'Convexity: curvature of the price/yield relationship. It makes the price fall (rates up) slightly smaller than the price rise (rates down).',
  dv01: 'DV01: price change (for the face value entered) when rates move by 0.01% (1 basis point).',
};

